Admin Portal — Coverage Analytics and Manual Report Export
Version 1 — Design baseline.
Contract baseline: AMVN-BE-2026-11-14-r2. Freeze scope: WF-01 through Admin Portal; implementation follows the owning Part B and shared bindings.
Implementation lookup: entities/worker fencing B.1; transitions B.2; endpoints B.3; snapshot/aggregation B.4; rendering B.5; jobs/outbox B.6; deployment limits/privacy B.7; failures B.8; acceptance B.9. WF1/WF2 remain the source-data owners. Historical publication time and snapshot-time geography have separate meanings stated in A.6/A.12.

Part A — Technical / Design Specification
A.1 Purpose, scope and definitions
A.1.1 Business goal
Turn published AccessMap accessibility data into clearly defined coverage statistics by administrative area and time period. An Admin can review and export CSV, PDF or GeoJSON for potential collaboration. Local authorities and advocacy organizations receive any file manually outside the platform; The Admin Portal does not email or provide a partner API.
A.1.2 What coverage means
Coverage describes the observed AccessMap dataset, not the real-world share of all accessible places. Distinguish count of published Locations, count with at least one current published LocationFeature, per-feature presence/limitation, evidence quality and data gaps. Make the eligible population, geography, time window, feature statuses, source freshness and denominator explicit in every chart and export. Do not label an unobserved area inaccessible.
A.1.3 Source of truth
WF-01 Location and current published LocationFeature/Version are authoritative for location and feature values. WF-02 provides non-authoritative freshness/advisory/dispute context; WF-03 search results and OpenStreetMap/Overpass discovery are not a source of published accessibility truth. WF-04 contributor scores are not a location-quality substitute. The Admin Portal reads these projections and never changes their states.
A.2 Actors and system boundaries
Admin signs in only through the ReactJS Admin Portal using an active account with isAdmin=true. Mobile login rejects Admin credentials; no ordinary mobile User session is issued. User (isAdmin=false) cannot establish a portal session. Each management request rechecks authoritative isAdmin, portal session identity/revocation and action permission. Logout, disablement or removal of isAdmin immediately denies old elevated tokens. Client flags, Origin and UI routes cannot grant permission. Reuse the User/auth record and non-null boolean isAdmin; no Role/UserRole/permission lookup schema is required. Admin selects scope, requests aggregation and previews the stored results before choosing a file format. The ASP.NET Core Analytics Service validates parameters and uses a durable Hangfire job to aggregate a consistent PostGIS snapshot. PostgreSQL stores report parameters, metrics and preview data. After review, Admin selects CSV, PDF or GeoJSON and requests export; a separate Hangfire render job creates the private artifact from that same stored snapshot. A ready export supplies an authorized download path. External sharing remains manual. Human access uses the WF-01 B.0.13 AdminPortal JWT/session policy, refresh-cookie rotation, CSRF controls and immediate revocation.
A.3 Logical architecture
ReactJS Admin Portal → ASP.NET Core report API → CoverageReportRequest and durable aggregation job. Hangfire reads a consistent snapshot, runs region density, feature-ratio and confidence-summary branches, stores merged metrics/warnings and exposes Generated preview data. No file format is required at this stage. After preview and review, Admin selects a format and calls Export. The API commits CoverageExportRequest plus durable render work and returns 202 Accepted. Hangfire renders from the reviewed stored snapshot, stores the private artifact, records successful export audit and marks the export Ready. Download access is authorized and audited before an expiring link is returned.
A.4 Preconditions, actions and outcomes
A.4.1 Preconditions
An authenticated Admin with Coverage.Read/Generate/Export rights supplies a valid supported region, time window and feature filters. No output format is selected for initial aggregation. Geometry and period limits are checked before work starts. Insufficient published data still produces a preview with warnings and explicit denominators. Export later requires a reviewed snapshot, supported selected format and purpose; snapshot data must still be retained.
A.4.2 Product action mapping
The Admin portal exposes generate, review and export as distinct actions. External sharing remains an accountable manual step outside the system.

A.4.3 Business rules and validation outcomes
Validate input before expensive PostGIS aggregation. Density, feature proportions and confidence summary use one consistent retained snapshot. Low sample warns without blocking a valid export. Required-branch error records retry/failure on CoverageJob and prevents Generated/review/export until recovery; never substitute zero or publish an incomplete report.

A.5 Report lifecycle
Report lifecycle: Requested → Aggregating → Generated → Reviewed → Exported → Archived, with optional SharedRecorded after Exported. Generated means stored metrics, warnings and preview are ready; no downloadable file is required. Reviewed records the Admin and snapshot approval. Format selection happens after preview. Each CoverageExportRequest has its own Queued → Rendering → Ready or Failed lifecycle with bounded retries. A successful artifact plus export audit permits Ready and the report's first Exported transition. B.2 additionally defines retention-only archive of expired unexported/generated/reviewed reports and terminal failed generations; no active job or live execution may be archived. Rendering failure leaves the reviewed report and any earlier ready exports intact. SharedRecorded is an Admin-entered manual handoff, never inferred from a link or download. Changed scope creates a new report; another format creates a new export request against the same snapshot.
A.6 Coverage calculations
A.6.1 Region and time semantics
Use the configured versioned administrative boundary and PostGIS geography(Point,4326) for one canonical eligible Location. On a shared boundary assign it once with the deterministic canonical RegionId rule. AsOf uses to as the required inclusive publication cutoff and requires from to be omitted/null; Period requires both from and to with from<to, selecting [from,to). Period mode uses [from,to) and counts the latest qualifying scoped publication per unit within that window; it is activity in the period, not total as-of stock. Include exact cutoff/window, boundary version and timezone in metadata. Deduplicate merged Locations; never count historical versions as extra current places. Snapshot and branch populations follow A.6.2/B.4.
Approved time interpretation: use canonical identity, visibility/status, pin/region membership and reviewed scope mapping as of report SnapshotAt; select published feature versions by the requested AsOf cutoff or Period [from,to). This is historical publication activity evaluated against the report-generation identity map, not reconstruction of the historic map. Persist that identity/position/status/scope map with retained snapshot inputs so later merge, hide or pin correction cannot alter the generated report. Disclose “Identity and geography as of snapshot; feature publications as of cutoff/in period” in preview and all exports. Exact historical identity/status/merge reconstruction is outside this baseline. Accept offset-bearing ISO-8601 instants, normalize to UTC and use the same persisted microsecond-precision values for validation, hashing and queries. Reject nonzero fractional precision beyond six digits instead of silently moving a boundary; offset-free/invalid times are 422 INVALID_TIME_RANGE. Never replace the requested cutoff with now or SnapshotAt. Apply the report's frozen identity/status map: Closed locations and RetiredAt/IsMergeRetired features are excluded from current populations. A replacement business has its own identity/evidence; historical reports/snapshots retain original identities and do not retroactively change denominators or prior contribution correctness. Re-render uses retained snapshots, not newly changed lifecycle data.
A.6.2 Independent aggregate branches
Density counts DISTINCT canonical eligible published LocationId and divides only by a known region-area denominator. Feature distribution counts current units at (LocationId, ScopeId, FeatureType), grouped by canonical feature/status; its eligible denominator uses that same scoped-feature grain and requested snapshot/filter. Missing observations are disclosed separately, not treated as negative or counted as a fabricated feature. Confidence summarizes current trusted scoped-feature confidence with sample/missing counts. Never aggregate conflicting floors into a whole-place accessibility guarantee. Run all three branches independently and merge only the same retained snapshot.
A.6.3 Missing data and caveats
If no eligible published location exists, return zero observed locations and insufficient_data; ratios whose denominator is zero are null, not 0%. When a minimum sample threshold is missed, keep the report/export but display a warning on preview and within each file. Distinguish Unknown, no feature observation, stale or disputed evidence, and genuine negative published feature status. A map heat layer visualizes AccessMap observation density, not universal accessibility.
A.7 Report contents and formats
Every format includes reportId, generated time, geographic boundary label/version, time semantics, filters, population/denominator definitions, snapshot cutoff, metric definitions, sample sizes, missing-data warnings and provenance. CSV contains aggregate region/feature rows with protected spreadsheet-safe cell encoding. PDF contains the same summary, tables, legends and a clearly labelled coverage map when geometry permits. GeoJSON contains only approved aggregate region features with safe properties; do not include contributor GPS, raw media, individual identity or private moderation notes.
A.8 Admin review, export and sharing
Preview displays the stored metrics, warnings, excluded population and generation outcome. Admin reviews this snapshot, then selects CSV/PDF/GeoJSON and purpose and presses Export. The API returns 202 with exportId and Queued; the portal polls export status. The worker renders only the stored approved aggregates and required boundary snapshot, never reruns live Location queries during export. Once Ready, an authorized audited download returns an expiring link. Retries reuse the same request/artifact identity. Another format can be requested against the same snapshot; new scope or fresher map data requires a new reportId. Manual sharing is recorded separately. Admin also receives prioritized WF-01 cases, WF-02 reports and coordinate correction requests. Ranking reputation does not alter queue order or confidence. Admin can inspect private Location metadata history and scoped feature versions; Guest and User see only approved redacted history. Aggregate feedback by district, feature and time to identify data quality and coverage work without equating report volume to proven barriers or inferring floors from GPS. Public map layers use bounded client queries and are separate from Admin coverage heat layers; exports retain provenance, window, sample threshold and privacy review. The Admin worklist reuses WF-01 submission review and WF-02 report/dispute APIs; it does not create a second moderation truth store. Queue priority uses urgency, age and evidence needs, not ranking reputation. WrongAddressOrPin cases use WF-02's versioned metadata correction and AccessScope entrance anchor approval. A separate quality-insights view distinguishes raw report counts from Admin-confirmed corrected locations/features and groups approved signals by district, scoped feature and period. The three-metric contract displays standing, contribution-only raw accuracy with assessed claim counts and ranking points separately. Comment reports reuse the WF2 report queue and comment moderation history. Admin-confirmed comment conduct violations affect only AccountStanding; neither report creation nor hiding alone triggers a penalty. Apply one sanction per confirmed violation and compensate an overturned decision once. No comment-report reward, report/comment accuracy input or confidence change is allowed. Admin uses WF1 per-claim assessments, WF2 versioned report outcomes and WF4 typed adjustments; never enter an arbitrary accuracy percentage. Coverage confidence discloses modes and missing counts, not null-as-zero.
A.9 Exception paths
Invalid region/time/filter: 400/422 before aggregation job creation. No data: Generated preview with insufficient_data and null ratios. Aggregate failures follow bounded retry on CoverageJob and block Generated/review/export; Incomplete reports are disabled. Export from an unreviewed report or unavailable retained snapshot: 409, no render job. Unsupported format: 422. A render/storage failure retries the export job, then marks that export Failed while preserving preview and earlier exports. Unauthorized preview/export/download: 403. Expired links can be reissued only for a retained Ready artifact with audit. Oversized scope requires narrowing or bounded partitioning.
A.10 Security and privacy
Use least-privilege admin scopes, rate limits and job quotas. Apply small-cell suppression or coarser region granularity for sensitive aggregates where policy requires, while explaining suppressed values. Enforce private storage, encrypted transport, file retention and signed-link expiry. Record generation and every export; avoid raw contributor/device GPS, identity, precise photo metadata, personal scores and object-storage secrets in reports. Review output before external manual sharing. Quality insights use aggregate-only read permission and an explicit minimum sample/small-cell policy; private per-report evidence is available only through its authorized case view. Public map layer payloads come from WF-03 bounded viewport APIs, not this Admin analytics endpoint.
A.11 Acceptance outcomes
A valid generation request yields a durable aggregation job and reproducible stored snapshot/preview without selecting a format or creating an artifact. Zero denominator is null with warning; low-data reports remain exportable after review. Export selects format after preview, returns 202, and creates one durable request per idempotency key. Rendering uses the reviewed snapshot even if live map data changes; no link is issued before Ready. Export audit precedes link delivery. Failed rendering preserves preview and permits another format request. Only an authorized Admin can access reports; no automatic partner API or email is invoked.
A.12 Reporting definitions and configuration
Use these reporting definitions consistently with A.6 and Part B. The existing regional density, scoped-feature proportions and confidence summary are the only metric branches. Required-branch aggregation failure blocks Generated/review/export; Incomplete output is disabled. Operational bounds, retention and privacy settings are explicit versioned deployment configuration, not new business features. Reject unconfigured reporting safely rather than assuming unlimited scope or bypassing suppression.

Part B — Implementation Specification
B.0 Implementation authority
Part B specifies the existing Analytics Service contract. It adds no external partner integration and never modifies WF-01/WF-02 source truth. A.6/A.12 define metric meaning; B.7 defines required deployment configuration and validation before operational reporting.
Cross-workflow implementation baseline: create this module within the new .NET8 Code First solution under WF1 B.0.3–B.0.13. Use the shared defined User/auth, DbContext, repository/UoW, storage, jobs and outbox once; there is no old solution or backfill. Apply Worker→Internal Job API, typed DTOs, configuration, DI, generic bases, scoped IUnitOfWork facade and executable acceptance. The owning Part B defines business policies, routes, final schema and retry budgets. Scenario IDs replace diagram-pair numbering. All SQL/EF business access is API-side; scheduler storage is separately credentialed. Include the module in S01–S08 checkpointed generation and the complete test matrix.
B.1 Data model and ERD
Complete the existing report/export entities as follows; no new analytics subsystem. Id fields use UUID v4, timestamps use timestamptz UTC, strings use text and JSON collections use jsonb. Fields are NOT NULL unless nullable is stated. Source/audit FKs restrict destructive deletion; retain existing xmin concurrency.
CoverageReportRequest: Id uuid PK; RequestedBy uuid FK to authorized User; CreatedAt timestamp; RegionId/BoundaryVersion text; From timestamptz nullable only for AsOf; To timestamptz NOT NULL for both modes; TimeMode enum; FeatureFilter jsonb; Status using existing report lifecycle; PolicyVersion/ParametersHash text; SnapshotAt timestamp fixed on the consistent aggregation snapshot; retained boundary/preview inputs jsonb; Purpose text. SnapshotAt/preview inputs may be null only before they exist; Generated requires all mandatory snapshot/preview data. Enforce a mode-aware check: AsOf requires From IS NULL and To IS NOT NULL; Period requires both values and From<To. Create valid mode-aware parameters from the initial request; no legacy cutoff/backfill is supported. No new asOfAt field or duplicate time column is introduced.
Obtain RequestedBy from auth. Canonical TimeMode wire names are AsOf and Period, retaining the existing cutoff versus [from,to) semantics in A.6/A.12. Canonical report Status values are Requested, Aggregating, Generated, Reviewed, Exported, SharedRecorded, Archived; transitions remain B.2. Do not invent Failed/Incomplete as report states. Approval storage: use the existing RetainedPreviewInputs jsonb, with immutable snapshot/boundary/preview data and a separate approval member {reviewedBy, reviewedAt, snapshotAt, parametersHash, policyVersion}. Manual sharing is recorded in a separate manualShares array in existing RetainedPreviewInputs JSON, preserving snapshot/preview and approval members. Each accepted record contains {recordId,recordedBy,recordedAt,purpose,reference}; ID, actor and time are server-derived, reference is optional bounded handoff context without private evidence. This records a manual action and sends no file. Aggregation/export hashes exclude approval and manualShares metadata.
Before review, approval is absent. On Generated→Reviewed, set reviewedBy from the authorized authenticated Admin and reviewedAt from server UTC; copy the exact stored SnapshotAt, ParametersHash and PolicyVersion. Persist approval and status atomically under xmin. Approval metadata is excluded from aggregation inputs/hashes and does not mutate the retained preview or snapshot identity. Never accept client-supplied reviewer/time or silently replace an approval; replay against the same approved snapshot returns the existing result, while stale or mismatching identity returns 409.
Export authorization requires this approval to match the retained snapshot in Reviewed, Exported or SharedRecorded; a changed scope requires a new report.
CoverageReportMetric: ReportId uuid FK; RegionId and MetricKey text; composite PK (ReportId,RegionId,MetricKey). MetricKey identifies the existing branch and feature/status grouping, preventing group overwrite. Numerator numeric>=0; Denominator numeric nullable and >=0 when present; SampleSize integer>=0; ValueNullable numeric nullable; WarningFlags jsonb; BranchStatus existing enum. Zero/missing denominator yields null ratio. BranchVersion in B.4 is the report's frozen metric PolicyVersion, not an additional column/entity. Upsert only the same report/snapshot/policy; incomplete branches cannot produce Generated. Canonical BranchStatus values are Pending, Succeeded, Failed: Pending is unfinished work, Succeeded means the branch result for the retained snapshot/policy is committed, Failed means unavailable due to recorded branch failure. Zero eligible data is Succeeded with warnings/null ratios, not Failed. Generated requires every mandatory branch Succeeded; failed branches never yield an incomplete public report. These labels normalize existing branch outcomes, not additional aggregation features.
CoverageExportRequest: Id uuid PK; ReportId uuid FK; RequestedBy uuid FK to authorized User; Format enum CSV/PDF/GeoJSON; Purpose/IdempotencyKey/RequestHash text; SnapshotAt timestamp and PolicyVersion text copied from reviewed report; Status Queued/Rendering/Ready/Failed; CreatedAt timestamp; CompletedAt timestamp nullable; FailureCode text nullable. UNIQUE (RequestedBy,ReportId,IdempotencyKey); changed payload conflicts. Ready/Failed require CompletedAt; Ready requires committed artifact/audit. Failure preserves the approved report.
CoverageArtifact: Id uuid PK; ReportId uuid FK; ExportRequestId uuid UNIQUE FK; Format enum; PrivateObjectKey/Sha256 text; Size bigint>=0; CreatedAt/RetentionUntil timestamps with RetentionUntil>CreatedAt. Report/format must match its export request. Keep objects private/immutable and retain source snapshot for the supported export period.
CoverageExportAudit: ExportRequestId uuid PK/FK for one successful render; ReportId/ArtifactId/AdminId uuid FKs; ExportedAt timestamp; Purpose/FileHash text. Values must match the Ready artifact/request; generation does not prove external sharing.
DownloadAccessAudit: ArtifactId/AdminId uuid FKs; AccessedAt timestamp; composite key (ArtifactId,AdminId,AccessedAt) for append-only access records. Check current authorization, Ready status and retention before issuing a link; repeated access remains auditable.
CoverageJob: Id uuid PK; Kind Aggregate/Render; ReportId uuid FK; ExportRequestId uuid nullable FK; Status reuses WF-01 AIProcessingJobStatus names and numeric identities: Queued=0, Processing=1, RetryScheduled=2, Succeeded=3, Failed=4, Cancelled=5. This Portal release uses Queued→Processing, Processing→RetryScheduled for a retryable failure, RetryScheduled→Processing when due, and Processing→Succeeded or Failed. Apply B.7 CoverageJobs options to both kinds: three total executions by default, with 30s then 120s backoff and a 300s lease. Claim/reclaim increments AttemptCount atomically only below MaxAttempts; an expired lease counts as the already-started execution. On retryable failure at attempt n<MaxAttempts, set NextRetryAt using RetryBackoffSeconds[n−1] from database current time and clear owner/lease. Nonretryable failure or exhaustion settles Failed without another execution. At default attempt 3, expired-lease recovery settles Failed and never starts a fourth execution. Preserve report/export behavior and fencing below; configure AutomaticRetry(Attempts=0) for the Hangfire handler so HTTP/scheduler retries cannot multiply the domain budget.
Cancelled remains a shared enum member but is not an enabled Portal transition or a new cancellation endpoint. AttemptCount integer>=0; LockedUntil/NextRetryAt timestamptz nullable; LockedByWorkerId varchar(100) nullable; FailureCode text nullable. CHECK Aggregate=>ExportRequestId IS NULL and Render=>ExportRequestId IS NOT NULL; a render export belongs to ReportId. Processing requires a live lease; RetryScheduled requires NextRetryAt; terminal states clear the lease and retry time. Only one nonterminal job per aggregation report/render export via existing active-status uniqueness and lease rules.
A recovered expired Processing lease resumes the same logical job under bounded retry; it does not create a second export. Aggregate Succeeded requires all mandatory branches/preview committed and report Generated; Render Succeeded requires Ready artifact/audit committed. Aggregate failure leaves the report ungenerated; render failure preserves its reviewed snapshot/earlier artifacts. Remote I/O remains outside DB transactions.
Execution fencing: claim/reclaim atomically sets Processing, increments AttemptCount and assigns LockedByWorkerId/LockedUntil. Before aggregate or render completion/failure/retry writes, lock the job and recheck Processing, matching captured AttemptCount and worker ID, and LockedUntil greater than database current time. Also recheck report/export state, snapshot and policy identity under their concurrency guards. A stale or expired worker performs no business/status/artifact/audit/outbox writes; cleanup only its unreferenced private temporary objects.
Recovery locks status/attempt/lease, settles exhausted expired work without a new execution, and clears owner/lease at terminal state. InitialCreate includes all owner/version/snapshot fields. An ownerless Processing job is invalid and reconciled safely, never accepted for completion.
B.2 States and transition guards
Requested→Aggregating when the durable aggregate job is claimed; Aggregating→Generated only after required branches, warnings and retained preview are persisted. Generated→Reviewed records authorized approval of the exact snapshot. After preview, an export request moves Queued→Rendering→Ready or Failed. Ready commits the artifact and CoverageExportAudit; the first Ready export moves the report Reviewed→Exported. A failed export preserves the approved report and earlier artifacts. Exported→SharedRecorded is a manual handoff; Exported/SharedRecorded→Archived follows retention; the final generation-outcome/recovery binding below also defines retention-only archive for terminal-failed Requested/Aggregating and expired Generated/Reviewed reports. Use existing xmin optimistic concurrency plus snapshot identity on report/export writes. Persist approval actor/time/snapshot identity in CoverageReportRequest.RetainedPreviewInputs.approval as defined in B.1, atomically with Reviewed. CoverageExportAudit records successful renders and DownloadAccessAudit records link/access activity; neither substitutes for snapshot approval. Reuse these existing structures; no transition-history table is introduced. Aggregation failure is recorded on the existing CoverageJob with failure/retry metadata and keeps this report ungenerated. Automatic retries remain within the original budget; a terminal failed generation is recovered only by an explicit new report request under the following contract. Incomplete reports are disabled.
Final generation-outcome and recovery binding: GET /api/v1/admin/coverage-reports/{id} returns the existing report status plus aggregation:{jobId,status,attemptCount,maxAttempts,nextRetryAt,failureCode,canRequestReplacement}. status uses the existing CoverageJobStatus; safe failureCode is nullable, and canRequestReplacement is true only for a terminal Failed Aggregate job on an unarchived report. HTTP200 describes the retained report even when that job failed; never disguise terminal aggregate failure as ongoing polling or add a Failed report state. Review/export remains forbidden until Generated/Reviewed. The UI may show the failure and offer a new generation request. Recovery after exhausted/nonretryable aggregation creates a new report with the same or narrowed parameters and a new Idempotency-Key; it never resets the old job, starts a fourth attempt or silently changes the report ID under a replayed key. No manual aggregate-retry endpoint is added.
Coverage-reconcile also performs bounded retention/archive work in the existing API-side maintenance task. Add CoverageReportRequest.ArchivedAt timestamptz NULL and ArchiveReason varchar(50) NULL; Archived requires both, other states keep them null. UnexportedReportRetentionDays=7 is a versioned default alongside B.7. Expiry is the later of CreatedAt+7d and every retained Ready artifact's RetentionUntil. A Requested/Aggregating report may archive only after its Aggregate job is terminal Failed; Generated/Reviewed/Exported/SharedRecorded may archive when expired. Every path requires no Queued/Processing/RetryScheduled aggregate/render job, no live receipt/execution and successful authoritative reference checks. Lock report/jobs/exports, recheck expiry/current versions, set Archived/ArchivedAt/ArchiveReason atomically. Thus these additional retention-only transitions into Archived are explicit; they do not enable approval, export or cancellation.
After archive no new review/render/download/link renewal is allowed; GET retains the authorized summary with archival time/reason. Retain report parameters, metrics/warnings, boundary/policy/snapshot hashes, approval/manual-share audit and artifact/audit metadata under project audit retention. Expired private export bytes and heavy render-only inputs may be removed only by reference-safe cleanup after archive/no live work; retain protected audit JSON members and record cleanup outcome. No physical cascade deletion of report/export/artifact/audit rows and no deletion based on an uncertain database result. Tests cover terminal failure DTO/new request, exact replay of the failed original request, no budget reset, unexported report expiry, a later export extending retention, active render blocking archive, concurrent download/archive, private-byte cleanup and retained audit.
B.3 Admin API
POST /api/v1/admin/coverage-reports accepts region, timeMode, from/to, featureTypes and purpose with Idempotency-Key; no formats field is required or consumed for rendering at this stage. Return 202 with reportId and Requested. GET /api/v1/admin/coverage-reports/{id} returns status, stored metrics, warnings and preview. POST /{id}/review records approval of that snapshot. After preview/review, POST /{id}/exports accepts {format, purpose} with its own Idempotency-Key; commit CoverageExportRequest and render job, return 202 {reportId, exportId, status: Queued, statusUrl}. GET /{id}/exports/{exportId} returns Queued/Rendering/Ready/Failed with a safe failureCode; only Ready returns artifactId and the authorized download endpoint. GET /{id}/downloads/{artifactId} checks isAdmin=true, validated Admin Portal session/scope, action rights and report/export ownership, report/export readiness and retention, audits access and issues an expiring link. Same export key/payload replays the original 202/exportId; a changed format or purpose with that key returns 409. Optional POST /{id}/manual-share-record accepts {purpose,reference?} with Idempotency-Key, checks an authorized retained report in Exported/SharedRecorded, appends one server-attributed manualShares record under xmin and sets SharedRecorded atomically. Exact replay returns that record; changed payload conflicts. It sends no file. GET /api/v1/admin/quality-insights?regionId&from&to&featureType returns bounded report counts by status/reason, confirmed metadata corrections, published scoped feature changes and missing-data counts with denominators, source/version and suppression flags. It never labels report volume as proven accessibility barriers. placeType is unsupported in this baseline because no authoritative persisted place-type catalogue is defined; reject it rather than infer a filter from a provider label. Supported insights group by region, feature and time. Worklist cards link to the existing WF-01 Admin endpoints, WF-02 reports/disputes and approved entrance metadata action rather than duplicating their transitions. Generation time subpayloads are {timeMode:"AsOf",from:null,to:"2026-09-01T00:00:00Z"} and {timeMode:"Period",from:"2026-08-01T00:00:00Z",to:"2026-09-01T00:00:00Z"}; all other existing region/filter/purpose fields remain unchanged. Missing to, non-null from for AsOf, missing Period endpoints, from>=to or invalid instants return 422 INVALID_TIME_RANGE; unknown timeMode returns 422 UNSUPPORTED_TIME_MODE before any report/job write. ParametersHash/request replay hash includes canonical TimeMode, explicit null for omitted AsOf from, normalized UTC endpoints and existing normalized filters/purpose. Equivalent offset spellings of the same instant hash identically; a different mode/window under the same key returns 409.

B.4 Validation and PostGIS aggregation
Reject invalid geometry, out-of-range time/radius, unsupported feature types and excessive complexity before enqueue. Query active/published canonical Locations using indexed spatial predicates, version-aware feature joins and partitioned result sets. Execute three logically separate branches with shared SnapshotAt and region version; persist each metric idempotently by (ReportId, RegionId, MetricKey) under the report's frozen PolicyVersion (the BranchVersion); retries cannot mix policies. Enforce query timeout and quota; a single giant SQL query is not required.
Implement the three branches against one PostgreSQL REPEATABLE READ snapshot in a bounded aggregation transaction, or materialize a versioned eligible-row snapshot first and run all branches from it. A shared timestamp string alone does not guarantee the same rows when WF-01 publishes concurrently. Persist a SnapshotToken/data cutoff, boundary version, branch input counts and metric policy version; on retry either reuse the materialized snapshot or restart all branches, never merge partial results from different snapshots.
Keep remote services and rendering outside the transaction.
Resolve eligible canonical Locations and non-retired scoped feature identities at SnapshotAt, with merge history mapping retained for prior published versions. For AsOf select each unit's latest eligible publication at or before request.To; for Period select its latest qualifying publication in [request.From,request.To). Do not substitute the live latest feature into an older window. Scope conflicts resolved by a merge retain the reviewed selection/provenance; count one canonical unit, not every alias. Snapshot inputs retain identity/geometry/visibility mapping and chosen feature/version IDs along with boundaries. SnapshotAt is assigned by the server's consistent input-snapshot acquisition, not accepted from the client and not substituted for To. Period bounds retain the existing configured MaxTimeRange rule.
The same disclosure and selection apply to all three branches and every export. Post-snapshot changes never mutate the stored report.
B.5 Rendering and object storage
Create no file during preview aggregation. The separate render worker loads the approved stored metrics, warning flags and retained boundary/format inputs using CoverageExportRequest.ReportId and frozen snapshot identity. Never query live Location rows again during export. Use safe CSV encoding, bounded PDF maps and aggregate GeoJSON. CoverageProcessingService calls the existing IObjectStorage.PutExportAsync(privateKey,artifact,ct) contract defined in WF-01 B.8.3. Use a deterministic private key bound to ExportRequestId, render JobId, captured attempt and format; repeat uploads within that attempt reuse only identical verified bytes/hash/type/size. A new fenced attempt gets its own key and cannot overwrite another attempt's bytes. Canonical CoverageArtifact.ExportRequestId uniqueness and fenced completion still permit only one Ready artifact for the export. Keep storage I/O outside DB transactions and reconcile orphan objects. A new format creates a new export request referencing the same snapshot; retain the source snapshot for the supported export period. Rendering uses retained snapshot timestamps/metadata for deterministic bytes. Enforce MaxArtifactBytes during generation and upload; a too-large artifact is a controlled render failure, not an unbounded memory buffer or truncated successful file. Only the live owner may attach uploaded metadata; cancellation/failed completion cleanup checks references before deleting its own orphan object.
B.6 Durable jobs and consistency
Generation commits CoverageReportRequest plus Aggregate domain job and returns 202; dispatch follows commit with reconciliation. CoverageJobHandler calls InternalJobApiClient.AggregateCoverageAsync→CoverageJobsController→ICoverageProcessingService. The API-side service claims and aggregates one consistent metric/preview snapshot and marks Generated. Export validates reviewed snapshot, format and payload-bound idempotency, commits CoverageExportRequest plus Render domain job and returns 202. The handler calls RenderCoverageAsync through the same API boundary. API-side CoverageProcessingService claims the lease, sets Rendering, constructs CoverageRenderInput from retained data and renders/uploads private bytes outside DB transactions. Only after B.1 owner/attempt/live-lease and snapshot fencing may it commit artifact metadata, successful export audit, Ready, any first Exported transition, job success and shared WF-01 B.3.17 outbox together. ExportReady uses sourceType=CoverageExportRequest, sourceId=export.Id, sourceEventId=CoverageExportAudit.ExportRequestId and authorized requesting Admin as recipient. Canonical Ready artifact/audit must exist; no SubmissionId/RelatedCaseId is required. Retry never reaggregates live data or duplicates artifact/audit. Exhaustion marks export Failed while preserving the reviewed report. Download audit commits before returning a signed link. WF-01 B.0.4 governs service authentication, request replay and timeouts; worker handlers have no business DbContext.
B.7 Business rules and configuration
Required versioned configuration: MaxArea (positive square kilometres), MaxTimeRange (positive duration), MaxRows (positive row count), MaxArtifactBytes (positive maximum encoded artifact bytes), MinSampleForHeadline (positive integer), SuppressSmallCells (explicit privacy policy), JobTimeout (positive duration), Concurrency (positive bounded job count), ExportLinkTTL (positive duration), RetentionDays (positive integer). CoverageJobs options use the same existing IOptions mechanism: MaxAttempts=3 total started executions (one initial plus at most two retries), RetryBackoffSeconds=[30,120], LeaseSeconds=300 and CompletionMarginSeconds=30. These are the explicit defaults for both Aggregate and Render, independent of WF-01's four-execution AI budget; production may override them only as validated versioned configuration. Require MaxAttempts>=1, exactly MaxAttempts−1 positive backoff entries, and LeaseSeconds>JobTimeout+CompletionMarginSeconds. Set the server work timeout to JobTimeout and the internal client/proxy total operation budget to at least JobTimeout+30s; HTTP retries stay inside that budget and do not increment AttemptCount. Validate at startup and before report work; no implicit unlimited/zero fallback. Deployment supplies tested values for the supported region/capacity. Missing/invalid settings leave coverage generation/export disabled with an explicit configuration error, without disabling other workflows. Link TTL cannot exceed remaining artifact retention; accepted jobs enforce area/time/row/timeout limits. Low sample warns rather than failing valid scope. For sensitive cells below the configured threshold, suppress exact numerator, denominator, sample count and derived value consistently; another format/total must not leak them. Policy version and snapshot travel with every artifact. Confidence uses 0–1; density is distinct eligible canonical places per square kilometre; feature proportions use the same (LocationId,ScopeId,FeatureType) population. Zero denominator yields null, not 0%. No branch declares universal/regulatory compliance. Development and acceptance fixtures supply these named settings explicitly through the shared configuration mechanism and test bounded work plus invalid/missing settings. Reuse the shared configuration subsystem; the job defaults above complete the existing bounded-retry requirement. B.7 is the sole configuration list for this workflow. MaxArtifactBytes applies independently to CSV, PDF and GeoJSON render streams and adapter writes; the greenfield baseline supplies the bounded defaults below; they are starting limits, not measured performance guarantees. A fixture explicitly sets 10485760 bytes and tests just below, at and above that cap. Oversize, hash mismatch, existing-object conflict or cancelled/partial upload never produces Ready.
Initial Demo/baseline configuration: MaxArea100 km²; MaxTimeRange31d; MaxRows10000; MaxArtifactBytes10485760; MinSampleForHeadline5; SuppressSmallCells=true; JobTimeout120s; Concurrency1; ExportLinkTTL300s; RetentionDays7 from Ready; UnexportedReportRetentionDays7 from CreatedAt with the documented active-job/artifact extensions. The same values seed integration fixtures, with explicit per-test overrides. Production overrides remain validated policy, never unlimited fallback. Retention starts at Ready; an open generation/render operation retains its own inputs until terminal/cleanup eligibility. These finite defaults remove an unspecified configuration decision from generation without claiming capacity was benchmarked.

B.8 Failure matrix
EX-01 invalid params →400/422, no job. EX-02 zero eligible data →Generated with warning and null ratios. EX-03 branch timeout/failure →bounded job retry; on exhaustion retain failure metadata, keep report ungenerated and block review/export; no Incomplete output. EX-04 render/storage error →bounded export retry, then export Failed while approved preview/earlier Ready files remain. EX-05 unauthorized →403 and audit. EX-06 expired link →new logged short-lived link if authorized. EX-07 oversized scope →422 requesting narrower scope. EX-08 sensitive small cell →existing suppression/coarsening policy and warning.

B.9 Test scenarios and Definition of Done
Test boundary/time semantics, merged-place dedup, current feature versions, zero denominator, warnings and branch reconciliation. Verify generation without formats produces preview without an artifact; selecting a format after review returns 202/exportId; polls expose Queued/Rendering/Ready/Failed; map updates between preview and export cannot alter exported figures; another format uses the same snapshot; key/payload replay returns the same export and changed payload returns 409; no link before Ready; render failure preserves preview; checksum, CSV safety, aggregate GeoJSON privacy, retention, signed-link expiry and audit ordering remain enforced. Storage contract fixtures exercise PutExportAsync on the common adapter/fake: successful upload and stored checksum; repeated identical key/content with no duplicate object; mismatching content under an existing key rejected without overwrite; actual stream exceeds MaxArtifactBytes despite dishonest metadata; a consumed/nonseekable stream requires a fresh source on retry; cancellation/partial multipart failure; upload success followed by failed/stale database completion; reference-safe orphan cleanup; and a stale attempt unable to overwrite/delete the winning Ready artifact.
Done means the preview and every exported format match the approved snapshot, and no system-to-partner transfer occurs. Test Guest public search/view without credentials, 401 for Guest writes/private data, User denial at portal login, Admin denial at mobile login, required isAdmin=true plus current portal session, logout/revocation and immediate denial after isAdmin removal. Test worklist ordering without a tier-based decision bypass, wrong-pin approval with immutable before/after history, quality-insights separation of Submitted reports from confirmed corrections, scope/floor grouping, small-cell suppression, stable bounded pagination and no private evidence in aggregate/map responses.
Add a concurrent WF-01 publication during aggregation: the three metric branches must reflect either the same before state or the same after state, never a mixture. Retry after one branch failure must preserve the chosen snapshot identity or start a new complete aggregation; preview and all export formats must retain identical counts and source versions. Verify missing/invalid required configuration disables only reporting work; zero-denominator/null and sensitive-cell suppression match preview/CSV/PDF/GeoJSON.
Check metric composite-key idempotency, single successful export audit, matching report/export/artifact references, and link TTL bounded by remaining retention. Verify approval and Reviewed commit atomically, the authenticated reviewer/time and exact snapshot identity are retained in the existing JSON, approval retries cannot replace that evidence, and missing/mismatching approval blocks export. Verify canonical job state/lease/retry guards in B.1, shared enum identity with WF-01, no Portal Cancelled transition, and no report Failed/Incomplete state.
Empty-data branches succeed with warnings; required-branch failure blocks generation. Job success must agree transactionally with Generated or Ready, according to job kind.
Historical mode fixtures: a pin move, hide or merge before report SnapshotAt changes identity/visibility/geography selection, while feature versions still follow AsOf cutoff or Period window. The same change after snapshot cannot change preview or any export. Assert disclosure of both time semantics, one canonical mapped scope-feature unit, archived source versions preserved, and no claim of historic-map reconstruction. Test AsOf with omitted/null from, required to and a publication exactly at to (included); Period includes exactly from and excludes exactly to. Reject extra/non-null AsOf from, missing endpoints, equal/reversed Period bounds, unknown mode, offset-free/nonfinite input and nonzero sub-microsecond precision before job creation. Equivalent UTC/offset inputs replay the same request; changed mode/instant conflicts. Stored parameters, preview, CSV/PDF/GeoJSON and retries must disclose/use identical normalized times while SnapshotAt remains independently server-owned.
Lease-fencing regression: pause worker A, expire/reclaim to worker B, then return A's aggregate/render result. A must commit no metrics, status, artifact metadata, successful audit or outbox; B alone may complete under matching owner/attempt/live lease and snapshot. Test the same ownership guard on retry/failure settlement, and exhausted recovery without another execution. Verify the default 3-execution budget, 30s/120s backoff, 300s lease and no fourth execution after crash/expiry; reject a lease not exceeding JobTimeout+CompletionMarginSeconds. Lost HTTP responses and repeated idempotency keys must neither increment attempts nor duplicate metrics/artifact/audit/outbox. Verify both Aggregate and Render, with scheduler AutomaticRetry disabled.
Assert ExportReady outbox accepts its validated CoverageExportRequest source with null SubmissionId/RelatedCaseId and notifies only its authorized requester. Repeated render completion reuses export identity/EventType and creates no duplicate notification; a foreign/missing/mismatched export source is rejected.
